<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edict of Torda</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1568">1568</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Edict of Turda</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Edict of Torda was a decree of the diet of the Eastern Hungarian Kingdom, meeting at Torda (Turda) in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a> in January 1568 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-sigismund-zapolya">John Sigismund Zápolya</a>. It allowed each community to choose preachers according to its own beliefs and forbade persecution on grounds of religion, at a time when anti-Trinitarian teaching was spreading at the prince's court. It is often cited as one of the earliest European laws of religious toleration.</p></div>
